ot a leave country that  hasn't changed one little bit and the  remain Parliament and a general election  could just sort this out how can you say  that nothing would have changed if this  deal goes through will take us out of  the customs union would take us out of  the free out of the single market it  would give us control over  free movement of people it would allow  us to strike our own trade deals I mean  it may not be the brexit that you want  but it is a form of bricks that come on  well on the first of November if we were  to leave we wake up and with stood in a  single market and we're still inside the  customs union and we're still paying  money to the EU because the transition  right and the transition okay last up  until the end of 2022 it's a very very  long way away any form the end of 2020  isn't it well and we have to give notice  of extension by the 1st of July the  chances of us sorting this out in the  next six months are just about zero and  as I say you know even even when you  hear the Prime Minister saying we take  back our fisheries read the small print  you know have a look at what is being  demanded by Monsieur Barnea in terms of  the level playing field basically he's  saying that in terms of employment  legislation social protection  environmental law and even taxation we  are gonna have to be on a level playing  field with the rest of Europe which  means we're not taking back control of  our laws this is not brexit and all the  dilemmas that we faced in the last few  months we'll be back with them in May  and June next year a clean break is the  only way we're going to get a proper  brexit just want to kind of reinforce  eyes that the end of the transition is  at the end of next year you've already  said that you don't actually really mind  an extension if it means very little  happening I want a general election so  an extension for a few weeks into which  we can have a general election is a much  better outcome than signing up to a  treaty that becomes part of  international law that binds us in  foreign policy and in many many other  areas this is a rotten deal if Boris  jobs that have presented this six months  ago to the House of Commons the ERG  every man and woman would have voted  against it I do understand because of  brexit fatigue and anger in the country  the temptation to vote for it but it is  nothing more than brexit in name only it  will not solve anything  this will not end things in fact in many  ways as far as Brussels are concerned  we've just finished the easy bit  I just wonder if you were acknowledged  that potentially public opinion appears  to be shifting slightly there was a Hugo  poll out this week for example which  found that two-thirds of people who  voted to leave in the referendum now  what the deal passed compared to 10% who  say that impatient vote against it so  you on the losing side well actually if  you look overnight you'll find YouGov  poll showing nearly 50% of people have  no idea what is actually in and let's  not call it a deal let's call it a  treaty because that's what it is  and I spoke to a lot of MPs on Friday  and Saturday and because they've been  bounced into this they didn't know what  was actually in these documents I urge  everybody particularly those in the ERG  and Euroskeptics I'm a Labour Party  spend today reading and understanding  the extent to which these documents bind  us in for the next few years and you  realize this is not brexit and the agony  will continue last time we spoke a few  weeks ago miss raj he said that you  would consider a pact with the  Conservative Party at the next election  now you've seen the details of the deal  that Boris Johnson has negotiated would  you still be open to that kind of deal  well the point about a general election  is it would give the Prime Minister the  chance to press the reset button the  chance to say actually you know mrs. Mae  tried with this treaty  I've amended 5% of it I've got some  